  "summary": "A unique blend of Portuguese and Indian architectural traditions in former Portuguese Goa.",
  "description": "Characterized by ornate facades, verandas, red-tiled roofs, and Manueline details adapted to tropical climate and local materials.",
  "culturalContext": "Goan Portuguese architecture (1510-1961) — a Luso-Indian hybrid unmatched in Asia: lime-plastered mansions of Fontainhas (Panjim), azulejo-tiled facades, oyster-shell window shells (jaali), Balcão sitting verandas, and baroque-gilded churches (Bom Jesus, UNESCO) against Indo-Portuguese furniture and rose windows. Old Goa's basilicas and convents were called \"Rome of the East\"; the susegad domestic style — chandeliers over red-oxide floors — survives in heritage homes and hoteliers' revival.",
